"""v2.1 -> v3 paper: ledger v4, Navier-Stokes viscous shock, T4 merged-block split.  Every number is filled from
tools/v4_numbers.py (results/*.json); the keys used are stored in tools/v4_paper_keys.json for check_paper_numbers."""
import json, string, sys
sys.path.insert(0, "tools"); import v4_numbers as V
N = V.numbers_v32(); N['m_vel_maxdiff_all'] = '%.0f' % max(float(N[k]) for k in N if k.endswith('_vel_maxdiff')); v10 = V.R("res_v10_t4")
b = v10["sb"]["band 0.15"]
N["t4_SB_imp_diff"] = "%.1f" % (100 * abs(b["impulsive"] / b["dns_del_impulsive"] - 1)); N["t4_SB_smo_diff"] = "%d" % round(100 * abs(b["smooth"] / b["dns_del_smooth"] - 1))
N["t4_RM_smooth_share"] = "%d" % round(100 * v10["rm"]["band 0.08"]["smooth"] / v10["rm"]["band 0.08"]["total"])
used = set()
import re
def F(s):
    def sub(m):
        k = m.group(1)
        if k not in N: return m.group(0)
        used.add(k); return N[k].replace("--", r"\mbox{--}")   # en dash in text and math mode
    return re.sub(r"\{([A-Za-z0-9_.]+)\}", sub, s)
p = "paper/paper.tex"; s = open(p).read()
def rep(old, new, count=1):
    global s
    assert s.count(old) >= 1, old[:60]; s = s.replace(old, new, count)
rep("Draft v2.1 (revised and verified), October 2026", "Draft v3.2 (ledger v4, viscous-shock DNS, merged-block circulation split), October 2026")
rep("where a point-wise tensor variant is second order.", F(r"""where a point-wise tensor variant is second order. A segment-wise estimator, which accepts the tensor increment of an anchor segment only where it is consistent with the polygon increment and evaluates stars cut by a sharp shock or contact on a one-sided arc, removes the error that is the ledger's own at shocks (blast-wave 95th percentile ${blast_p95_v2}\%\to{blast_p95_v4}\%$); geometric-window detection extends the telescoping to resolved viscous shocks computed by Navier--Stokes DNS; and splitting the circulation budget on each triangle's own merged shock block gives impulsive and smooth parts whose sum is exact; in three DNS both parts agree with the DNS velocity circulation of the same material triangles to within ${m_vel_maxdiff_all}\%$, and per unit interface length it follows the Samtaney--Zabusky local law only on the upstream $45^\circ$. A tetrahedral ledger is exact across manufactured three-dimensional planar shocks."""))
rep("which rejects roll-up and shear.", r"""which rejects roll-up and shear. For resolved shocks that need more than six snapshots, ledger v4 (Sec.~\ref{sec:v4}) uses windows $w\in\{2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,32\}$; a window is used only where hot steps inside it explain less than half of its area drop, and only the steps that carry the compression (single-step log ratio below $0.1$ of the window mean) are marked; the marked span is then extended on either side for as long as the star keeps compressing measurably (single-step log ratio below $-\max(3\hat\sigma_R,0.02R_{\rm peak})$, with $\hat\sigma_R$ a robust background of the tracer's single-step ratios and $R_{\rm peak}$ the largest compression in the window), so that the tails of a resolved profile are covered while a shock crossed within one snapshot does not lengthen the deferral.""")
rep(r"\section{Mass-metric Dirac operator}", r"""\subsection{Segment-wise estimator (ledger v4)}\label{sec:v4}
The hybrid of Sec.~\ref{sec:ledger} compares the two estimators only at the final time, so one bad tensor increment removes the tensor for the rest of the record. The ledger is a sum of anchor-segment increments, and both estimators can be evaluated on every segment with the same anchor. Ledger v4 uses the tensor increment of a segment only if the fit is well conditioned (relative standard error of $F$ below $0.05$), its neighbours do not straddle a material interface (a jump of the reference density by more than $0.1$ in $\ln$), and it differs from the polygon increment by at most $0.1$ in $\ln J$ on segments without hot steps, or by at most $0.02+2|\delta|+3\hat\sigma/h$ on segments with hot steps, where $\delta$ is the polygon non-affinity of the segment and $\hat\sigma$ the noise estimated from third time differences. Where the polygon is provably exact ($\delta\approx0$, planar shocks) the tensor must agree tightly; where the star moved non-affinely (vortex cores swept by a shock) the tensor may correct it. A rejected increment is replaced by the polygon increment of that segment alone, so outliers do not propagate. Two one-sided repairs treat the cases in which a star average is not a point value. A star that straddles a material interface is evaluated on the fan of the largest contiguous arc of same-material ring neighbours. A star cut by a shock at the evaluation time is evaluated on the longest ring arc that moves affinely with the centre: its least-squares affine residual must be below a quarter of that of the full ring and below $0.01+3\hat\sigma/h$, and the full-ring residual must exceed $0.03$. For a sharp shock or contact both repairs are exact, because the map from the anchor to the current time is affine on either side; for resolved shocks or smooth non-affine motion no arc qualifies and nothing changes. Masses are still taken from the polygon ledger.

\section{Mass-metric Dirac operator}""")
rows = "\n".join("%s & %s & %s & %s\\\\" % r for r in V.table_rows())
rep("the 95th percentiles at shocks are unchanged.", F(r"""the 95th percentiles at shocks are unchanged. Ledger v4 (Table~\ref{tab:v4}, Fig.~\ref{fig:v4}a) removes the part of the shock error that is the ledger's: the planar manufactured shock stopped mid-domain goes from ${planar_mid_p95_v2}\%$ (v2) and ${planar_mid_p95_v3}\%$ (hybrid) to exact, the blast wave from ${blast_p95_v2}\%$ to ${blast_p95_v4}\%$, Sod to ${sod_p95_v4}\%$, the shock--vortex DNS to ${sv_v4}\%$ (hybrid 95th percentile ${sv_p95_v3}\%$) and the viscous shock--bubble 95th percentile to ${sbv_p95_v4}\%$. The 95th percentiles of $30$--$35\%$ that remain in the inviscid shock--bubble and Richtmyer--Meshkov DNS belong to the reference: they lie on tracers whose fluid the DNS has numerically mixed with the other gas (passive scalar changed by more than $0.01$), where the DNS density is a mixed value. On the remaining tracers (${sb_clean_frac}\%$, ${rm_clean_frac}\%$ and ${sbv_clean_frac}\%$) v4 gives ${sb_clean_v4}\%$, ${rm_clean_v4}\%$ and ${sbv_clean_v4}\%$, against ${sb_clean_v2}\%$, ${rm_clean_v2}\%$ and ${sbv_clean_v2}\%$ for v2. With $0.02h$ noise v4 gives ${sod_noise_v4}\%$ (Sod), ${blast_noise_v4}\%$ (blast), ${sv_noise_v4}\%$ (shock--vortex) and ${rm_noise_v4}\%$ (RM): medians within $0.11$ percentage points of the hybrid's, a blast-wave 95th percentile a third of the hybrid's ($34\%$) and a slightly higher one in Sod ($3.3\%$ for the hybrid). With $0.02h$ noise the clean shock--bubble tracers give ${sb_noise_clean_v4}\%$ (v2: ${sb_noise_clean_v2}\%$) and the viscous ones ${sbv_noise_clean_v4}\%$ (v2: ${sbv_noise_clean_v2}\%$). The polygon ledger itself is not degraded by the new detection: with a deferral of ${sb_defer_v4}$ blocks it gives ${sb_poly_clean_v4}\%$ on the clean shock--bubble tracers against ${sb_poly_clean_v2}\%$ with v2 detection (all tracers: ${sb_poly_p95_v4}\%$ against ${sb_poly_p95_v2}\%$ at the 95th percentile, the difference lying on tracers with a mixed reference)."""))
V4FLOAT = ((r"""
\begin{table}[t]\centering\small\caption{Density from positions, ledger versions: median / 95th percentile of the relative error (\%) at the final time. Clean tracers: DNS passive scalar unchanged ($|\Delta Y_1|\le0.01$).}\label{tab:v4}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\toprule
Flow & v2 & v3 hybrid & v4\\\midrule
""") + rows + r"""
\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_v4.pdf}
\caption{(a) 95th percentile of the final-time density error for ledgers v2, v3 (hybrid) and v4. (b) Resolved viscous planar shocks from Navier--Stokes DNS: median error once every tracer has crossed; numbers above the grey bars are the single-step detection rates. (c) Circulation budget of the wide material band of the shock--bubble split on merged blocks (solid) against the DNS velocity circulation of the same triangles (dashed) and the DNS baroclinic torque integrated at every solver step (dash-dotted), with the same classification; dotted: the earlier split, which coincides with the total. (d) Impulsive deposition per unit initial interface length against the angle from the upstream point, with the SZ1 local law.}\label{fig:v4}\end{figure}""")
i = s.index(r"\label{tab:density}"); j = s.index(r"\end{table}", i) + len(r"\end{table}"); s = s[:j] + V4FLOAT + s[j:]
rep("while a wide band does not.", F(r"""while a wide band does not.

\paragraph{Impulsive and smooth deposition.} The split of the circulation budget into an impulsive and a smooth part is now defined on merged blocks. A triangle is hot at snapshot $n$ if a vertex is hot on step $n-1$ or $n$; over each of its hot blocks $[n_0,n_1]$ Theorem~\ref{thm:kelvin} telescopes, so the impulsive deposition of the triangle is $\Gamma_t^{n_1+1/2}-\Gamma_t^{n_0-1/2}$, a quantity of the block end frames. Blocks that do not compress the triangle (by more than $0.15$ in $\ln$) are not counted: in a rolled-up sheet neighbouring triangles exchange circulation through numerical or physical viscous fluxes. Every kept block is widened by one snapshot on either side. Impulsive plus smooth equals the change of the region circulation exactly (closure ${t4_SB_closure}$). The earlier split, which booked every snapshot with a shock anywhere in the region as impulsive, assigns the whole budget to the impulsive part here, because some part of the band is always being crossed. The primary reference is the DNS velocity circulation of the same material triangles at the frames (the solver velocity is stored at the tracers), split on the same blocks with the hat kernel of the position form, $\Delta\Gamma^n_{\rm DNS}=\tfrac12(\Gamma_{n+1}-\Gamma_{n-1})$; its total follows the position form to ${m_SB_vel_total}\%$ (median for $t>0.4$; narrow band ${m_SB8_vel_total}\%$, because it contains the numerical dissipation). For the wide band of the inviscid shock--bubble, positions give $|\Delta\Gamma|={m_SB_total}={m_SB_impulsive}+{m_SB_smooth}$ (impulsive + smooth) and the DNS velocity ${m_SB_vel_imp}+{m_SB_vel_smo}$; at the end of shock passage ($t=0.6$) ${m_SB_pass_impulsive}+{m_SB_pass_smooth}$ against ${m_SB_pass_vel_imp}+{m_SB_pass_vel_smo}$. The viscous shock--bubble gives ${m_SBV_impulsive}+{m_SBV_smooth}$ against ${m_SBV_vel_imp}+{m_SBV_vel_smo}$, and RM ${m_RM_impulsive}{m_RM_smooth}$ against ${m_RM_vel_imp}{m_RM_vel_smo}$; over all six bands (two per flow) the largest difference of either part is ${m_vel_maxdiff_all}\%$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:v4}c). The one-snapshot margin is what makes the split independent of the reference: without it the position form gives ${mg0_pos}$ and the DNS velocity ${mg0_vel}$ for the impulsive part of the wide band, because a captured shock reaches a triangle about one snapshot before its star crosses the detection threshold; with one snapshot ${mg1_pos}$ and ${mg1_vel}$, with two ${mg2_pos}$ and ${mg2_vel}$. Halving the snapshot rate changes the position-form impulsive part by $0.5\%$ (${dt04_pos}$ at $\Delta t=0.04$, no margin). Three references built from the acceleration instead of the velocity bracket the same values but carry an error of their own of a few per cent, set by the captured shocks: the line integral of the DNS acceleration $-\nabla p/\rho\,(+\nabla\!\cdot\!\tau/\rho)$ around the same triangles, integrated at every solver step with the same kernel (${m_SB_mat_imp}+{m_SB_mat_smo}$; its total is off by ${m_SB_mat_total}\%$), the baroclinic torque integrated at every solver step (${m_SB_step_imp}+{m_SB_step_smo}$), and the torque sampled at the snapshots. About a third of the circulation of the shocked bubble is thus deposited outside the triangles' own shock blocks, by the post-shock pressure field, whereas in RM the smooth part is a small negative correction from the reverberating waves. A P1 quadrature of the torque on the material triangles themselves fails once they are stretched (it changes sign late), as the vertex-vorticity quadrature did.

\paragraph{Comparison with the local deposition law.} Binning the impulsive deposition of the wide band by the initial angle $\theta$ from the upstream point and dividing by $R\,\Delta\theta$ gives the deposition per unit initial interface length (Fig.~\ref{fig:v4}d). The SZ1 model behind the total of Sec.~\ref{sec:circ} assumes $\Gamma'_1\sin\theta$ on the upstream quarter and $\Gamma'_1$ on the downstream quarter. The measured profile follows $\sin\theta$ on the first $45^\circ$ (${sz_bins3}$ against ${sz_law3}$, in units of $\Gamma'_1$), lies below it from $45^\circ$ to $90^\circ$ (${sz_bins_mid}$ against ${sz_law_mid}$), and falls to zero at the downstream stagnation point instead of staying at $\Gamma'_1$. Integrated, the impulsive deposition is ${sz_up}$ on the upstream quarter (SZ phase ii: ${sz_up_SZ}$) and ${sz_down}$ on the downstream quarter (SZ: ${sz_down_SZ}$; viscous flow: ${szv_up}$ and ${szv_down}$). The total, ${sz_total}$, is close to the SZ1 value ${sz_total_SZ}$ only because the smooth deposition makes up the downstream deficit: the SZ1 downstream term stands for impulsive and later deposition together. Per angular bin the smooth part is not meaningful, since it is dominated by circulation carried along the sheet between neighbouring triangles."""))
rep("(f) A thin shear layer quantifies the information limit of the stretch estimate.", F(r"""(f) A thin shear layer quantifies the information limit of the stretch estimate.

\paragraph{Resolved viscous shocks (Navier--Stokes DNS).} A planar viscous shock is one-dimensional, so it was computed by a one-dimensional Navier--Stokes DNS with the scheme and constant-viscosity model of the shock--bubble DNS (MUSCL--HLLC, $\mathrm{Pr}=0.72$, explicit viscous step limit, grid spacing $\delta/10$), with tracers advanced in the solver and embedded in two dimensions by a rotation ($\theta=0.5$) and a uniform tangential slip; the measured shock thickness is $\delta/h={visc_delta_range}$. Once every tracer has crossed the shock ($M_s=2.24$, $\eta=3$; Fig.~\ref{fig:v4}b) the single-step test detects a thin slow shock on ${visc_thin_det_v2}\%$ of the tracers and a thick one on none (errors ${visc_thin_v2}\%$ and ${visc_thick_v2}\%$). The six-snapshot windows of v3 detect the $M_s=2.24$ shocks but defer too briefly for the thick, slow one (${visc_thick_v3}\%$), and they miss the weak, thick, slow shock entirely ($M_s=1.5$, $\delta/h=4$, $C_s=0.25$: detection ${visc_weak2_det_v3}\%$, error ${visc_weak2_v3}\%$; v2 ${visc_weak2_v2}\%$). With geometric windows and adaptive tails the median error is ${visc_v4_range}\%$ in all six cases (thick, slow shock ${visc_thick_v4}\%$; weak shocks ${visc_weak_v4}\%$ and ${visc_weak2_v4}\%$); on the thin shocks v3 and v4 differ by at most $0.001$ percentage points (${visc_thin_v3}\%$ and ${visc_thin_v4}\%$). Mid-crossing, the error outside the shock layer is ${visc_mid_out}\%$. A fixed margin of half the marked span, used before the adaptive tails, gave $0.133\%$ on the weak shock."""))
rep("The multi-window shock test restores exactness on manufactured weak and slow shocks but has not been tested on resolved viscous shocks.",
    "Calibration distortions enter $\\ln J$ through their divergence and are not reduced by the ledger, and temporally correlated noise is underestimated by the third-difference noise estimate.")
rep("The method is robust to realistic position noise", "The segment-wise ledger removes the ledger's own error at shocks and telescopes across resolved viscous shocks, and the merged-block split separates impulsive from smooth circulation deposition exactly and in agreement with the DNS torque. The method is robust to realistic position noise")
rep("A fixed margin of half the marked span, used before the adaptive tails, gave $0.133\\%$ on the weak shock.", F(r"""A fixed margin of half the marked span, used before the adaptive tails, gave $0.133\%$ on the weak shock.

\paragraph{Three dimensions (manufactured maps).} The ledger carries over to the tetrahedral Delaunay complex unchanged: star volume instead of star area, the same deferral, and Proposition~2 holds because the volume ratio of a polyhedron under an affine map is $\det F$ (\texttt{lhd/ledger3d.py}). On three-dimensional planar manufactured shocks with an oblique normal, tangential slip and $\eta=1.5,3,6$ (${d3_n}$ interior tracers) it is exact to ${d3_exact}$ with deferral and errs by ${d3_nodefer}\%$ without; on a smooth compressible rotational map the local orders between successive spacings are ${d3_slopes}$, tending to first order as in two dimensions. No three-dimensional flow has been computed."""))
rep("Keeping one tracer in four or one in sixteen gives median errors of $0.85\\%$ and $1.8\\%$ in the shock--vortex flow.", F(r"""Keeping one tracer in four or one in sixteen gives median errors of $0.85\%$ and $1.8\%$ in the shock--vortex flow.

\paragraph{Synthetic particle-tracking errors.} Real particle-tracking data are not available, so their error structure was imitated on the shock--vortex DNS tracers with $\sigma=0.02h$ (\texttt{scripts/v13\_ptv\_synth.py}; v4, median / 95th percentile). White noise gives ${ptv_white}\%$. Spatially correlated noise is less harmful, because the star area depends on relative positions: ${ptv_spatial3}\%$ and ${ptv_spatial8}\%$ for correlation lengths $3h$ and $8h$. Temporally correlated noise (AR(1), coefficient $0.9$) gives ${ptv_temporal}\%$, but the third-difference noise estimate sees only $\hat\sigma/h={ptv_temporal_sighat}$. Seeding whose density varies by a factor of $3.3$ gives ${ptv_seeding}\%$, no worse than uniform seeding. A static calibration distortion of rms $0.1h$ and correlation length $10h$ gives ${ptv_calib}\%$ and is invisible to the noise estimate ($\hat\sigma/h={ptv_calib_sighat}$): it enters $\ln J$ as the change of its divergence along the trajectory, which no position-only estimator can remove, so calibration must bound the divergence of the distortion field directly. A position-only self-calibration was tried: on segments where a star translates without deforming, the measured area change is the change of $\nabla\!\cdot\!d$, which was fitted on a regularised grid (${sc_segments}$ segments). It fails here: the segment equations correlate only ${sc_corr}$ with the true values, because the post-shock flow translates in one direction only, and the inversion amplifies their error (${sc_after}\%$ after correction against ${sc_before}\%$ before; the exact correction would give ${sc_oracle}\%$). The distortion therefore has to be removed where the particle images are processed."""))
rep("All results are two-dimensional; the identities extend to tetrahedra and the three-dimensional Nanson formula, but no three-dimensional case has been computed.", "Apart from the tetrahedral ledger on manufactured maps, all results are two-dimensional; no three-dimensional flow has been computed.")
rep("Real particle-tracking data, with their correlated errors and seeding inhomogeneities, have not yet been analysed.", "Real particle-tracking data have not yet been analysed; only synthetic error models have.")

